    title: "PortSwigger Web Academy: XSS - 3 labs",
    description:
      "Complete three PortSwigger Web Academy labs covering the three fundamental XSS delivery mechanisms — reflected, stored, and DOM-based — each hosted on PortSwigger's own cloud infrastructure with a real vulnerable target and an automatic 'solved' check, so you practice against a realistic app rather than a static exercise. The DOM lab hinges on a real vulnerable sink — a trackSearch(query) function that calls document.write('<img src=\"/resources/images/tracker.gif?searchTerms=' + query + '\">') using query read straight from location.search via URLSearchParams, with no validation or encoding — so solving it means breaking out of that img src attribute rather than just closing a <script> tag.",
    platform: "PortSwigger",
    difficulty: "medium",
    estimatedHours: 3,
    instructions: [
      "Open the lab 'Reflected XSS into HTML context with nothing encoded' at portswigger.net/web-security/cross-site-scripting/reflected/lab-html-context-nothing-encoded and click 'Access the lab'",
      "In the search box, submit <script>alert(1)</script> and confirm the alert fires and the lab banner turns green",
      "Open 'Stored XSS into HTML context with nothing encoded' at .../cross-site-scripting/stored/lab-html-context-nothing-encoded",
      "Post a comment containing <script>alert(document.cookie)</script> and reload the blog post page to confirm the script fires for every visitor, not just you",
      "Open 'DOM XSS in document.write sink using source location.search' at .../cross-site-scripting/dom-based/lab-document-write-sink",
      "Use browser DevTools to find the vulnerable JavaScript: function trackSearch(query) { document.write('<img src=\"/resources/images/tracker.gif?searchTerms=' + query + '\">'); } with query read directly from location.search — note it lands inside an <img src=...> attribute with no encoding",
      "Craft the breakout payload \"><img src=x onerror=alert('XSS_doomed')> — it closes the existing src attribute, injects a new <img> element, and fires JavaScript through the onerror handler when the bogus image fails to load — and pass it via the URL's ?search= parameter to trigger the DOM sink",
      "For each of the 3 labs, record the exact sink (innerHTML, document.write) and why the specific payload was required to escape that context",
      "Screenshot each lab's green 'Congratulations, you solved this lab!' confirmation banner",
    ],
    resources: [
      {
        title: "Lab: Reflected XSS into HTML context with nothing encoded",
        url: "https://portswigger.net/web-security/cross-site-scripting/reflected/lab-html-context-nothing-encoded",
        type: "platform",
      },
      {
        title: "Lab: Stored XSS into HTML context with nothing encoded",
        url: "https://portswigger.net/web-security/cross-site-scripting/stored/lab-html-context-nothing-encoded",
        type: "platform",
      },
      {
        title:
          "Lab: DOM XSS in document.write sink using source location.search",
        url: "https://portswigger.net/web-security/cross-site-scripting/dom-based/lab-document-write-sink",
        type: "platform",
      },
      {
        title: "PortSwigger Web Academy: Cross-site scripting",
        url: "https://portswigger.net/web-security/cross-site-scripting",
        type: "link",
      },
    ],
    completionIndicator: `PortSwigger green 'Congratulations, you solved this lab!' banner captured individually for each of the three named labs (reflected, stored, DOM-based).`,
    slides: [
      {
        title: "Reflected XSS into HTML context",
        body: "On the 'Reflected XSS into HTML context with nothing encoded' lab, submitting <script>alert(1)</script> in the search box reflects the payload straight into the HTML with no encoding, firing the alert and turning the lab banner green.",
        diagram: {
          type: "request-response",
          leftLabel: "Browser",
          rightLabel: "PortSwigger reflected-XSS lab",
          exchanges: [
            {
              direction: "right",
              label: "GET /?search=<script>alert(1)</script>",
              payload: "<script>alert(1)</script>",
            },
            {
              direction: "left",
              label: "200 OK",
              payload: "payload reflected unencoded into page HTML",
            },
          ],
        },
      },
      {
        title: "Stored XSS in a blog comment",
        body: "On the 'Stored XSS into HTML context with nothing encoded' lab, posting a comment containing <script>alert(document.cookie)</script> persists the payload server-side, so it fires for every visitor who reloads that blog post -- not just the person who posted it.",
        diagram: {
          type: "browser-mockup",
          url: "/post?postId=1",
          annotations: [
            {
              label: "Comment field",
              note: "<script>alert(document.cookie)</script> submitted as a normal comment",
            },
            {
              label: "On reload",
              note: "script executes automatically for any visitor viewing the post",
            },
          ],
        },
      },
      {
        title: "Break out of the document.write sink",
        body: "DevTools reveals the vulnerable sink: trackSearch(query) calls document.write('<img src=\"/resources/images/tracker.gif?searchTerms=' + query + '\">') with query read straight from location.search. Passing \"><img src=x onerror=alert('XSS_doomed')> via ?search= closes the existing src attribute and fires JavaScript through the onerror handler.",
        diagram: {
          type: "flow-chain",
          steps: [
            {
              label: "location.search",
              detail: "?search=\"><img src=x onerror=alert('XSS_doomed')>",
            },
            {
              label: "trackSearch(query)",
              detail:
                "query passed straight into document.write with no encoding",
            },
            {
              label: "document.write(...)",
              detail:
                'writes <img src="...searchTerms="><img src=x onerror=alert(\'XSS_doomed\')>">',
            },
            {
              label: "onerror fires",
              detail: "bogus image fails to load, alert('XSS_doomed') executes",
            },
          ],
        },
      },
    ],
  },
