  {
    id: "L26",
    number: 26,
    week: 9,
    moduleId: "m9",
    title: "PortSwigger Web Academy: Broken Access Control - 2 labs",
    description: `Complete two PortSwigger Web Academy access-control labs that show the two most common ways authorization checks fail in real apps: an IDOR where a predictable filename exposes another user's private data, and an admin panel that is reachable simply because its URL was never actually access-controlled, only hidden.`,
    platform: "PortSwigger",
    difficulty: "medium",
    estimatedHours: 3,
    instructions: [
      `Open 'Insecure direct object references' at portswigger.net/web-security/access-control/lab-insecure-direct-object-references`,
      `Use the Live chat widget, send any message, then click 'View transcript' and note the resulting URL ends in a numbered filename such as /chat/2.txt`,
      `Change the number in the URL down to a lower value (e.g. /chat/1.txt) to read another user's (carlos's) chat transcript via direct object reference`,
      `Extract carlos's password from the leaked transcript text and use it to log in as carlos to solve the lab`,
      `Open 'Unprotected admin functionality' at portswigger.net/web-security/access-control/lab-unprotected-admin-functionality`,
      `Append /robots.txt to the lab's base URL and read the Disallow: entry, which discloses the hidden admin panel's path`,
      `Navigate directly to the disclosed admin panel URL without ever logging in as an administrator`,
      `Use the panel's exposed 'Delete user' action to delete the user carlos, which solves the lab`,
      `Screenshot the green completion banner for both labs`,
    ],
    resources: [
