  {
    id: "L31",
    number: 31,
    week: 10,
    moduleId: "m10",
    title: "PortSwigger: SSTI Challenge",
    description: `Fingerprint a server-side templating engine and escalate from a math-evaluation proof to full remote code execution across two PortSwigger Web Academy SSTI labs -- the skill of recognizing when user input is being rendered as template syntax rather than plain data, and pivoting to the engine-specific RCE gadget once it's confirmed.`,
    platform: "PortSwigger",
    difficulty: "hard",
    estimatedHours: 3,
    instructions: [
      `Open 'Basic server-side template injection' and log in with wiener:peter; locate the input field that is echoed back on the page (e.g. a 'message of the day' or product review comment)`,
      `Fuzz the field with a polyglot payload such as \${{<%[%'"}}%\\ and observe the resulting error message to identify hints about the templating engine in use`,
      `Confirm real template evaluation (not just reflection) with a math payload such as {{7*7}} -- if 49 appears in the response, this is likely the Jinja2/Twig-style \${...} or {{...}} family and not a Freemarker/Velocity variant which uses different syntax`,
      `Escalate to RCE using the engine's known gadget, e.g. for Jinja2: {{ self.__init__.__globals__.__builtins__.__import__('os').popen('id').read() }}; use it to delete the file /home/carlos/morale.txt as required by the lab, then confirm the lab is marked solved`,
      `Open 'Server-side template injection using documentation'; when the fuzz payload errors out with a named engine and version but no public gadget is memorized, locate that engine's official documentation to find an expression capable of calling into the underlying language runtime`,
      `Adapt the documented expression syntax (not a copy-pasted generic payload) to reach a Java/Python/Ruby native call, execute a command such as id or delete the required file, and confirm the lab is solved`,
      `For both labs, record the exact fingerprinting payload, the engine name you identified, and the final gadget used, since the same escalation path (detect -> confirm -> engine-specific RCE) transfers directly to real SSTI findings`,
